  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nDevelopment of a deep trough over the eastern states will drive\nmuch cooler air down into our area starting Thursday. There will\nalso be a series of weak waves that could extend scattered shower\nchances through Thursday into early Friday, mainly for the Upper \nCumberland. It is not out of the question that a snowflake could \nfall on the high Plateau late this week. If so, it will be of no \nconsequence, so let's just keep that between you and me.\n\nReally the main story here is the incoming cold snap. Temperatures\nwill hold in the 50s Thursday with northwest winds gusting to 25\nmph at times. Highs will be limited to the 40s to lower 50s for \nFriday and Saturday. This is 10 to 15 degrees below normal. Nights\nwill be quite cold with widespread 20s Friday night and Saturday \nnight. This will likely be Nashville's first freeze of the season \nwhich will be a little later than the average first freeze of \nNovember 1st. With a dry pattern, temperatures will slowly \nrecover after Saturday, reaching normal levels by the middle of \nnext week.",
